  "slug": "report-technical-4b00",
  "summary": "Recent DDI, virus-inhibitor, CRISPR and PPI studies report accuracy while a new benchmark shows representation rankings depend on split regime.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is how predictive performance in biomolecular tasks is validated. The DrugBank-derived drug-drug interaction preprint tests four chemical-overlap holdout regimes and finds that molecular representation rankings shift with the split, so a single benchmark setting can misrank methods. The published items are mainly model-development reports: DeepMolVir encodes viruses with ESMC-600M embeddings for compound-virus activity prediction, PriMPT models guide RNA-DNA pairs for CRISPR/Cas9 off-target cleavage, PertiNet fuses sequence, structure and GO annotations for protein-protein interaction prediction, and the MeSH-knowledge-graph DDI study reports AUROC 0.988 transductive and 0.937 in a second setting. What is new is the emphasis on split design and reliability over headline accuracy.",
